VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-001
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful nodulocystic facial flare with focal purulent discharge and escalating local erythema.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of severe facial pain and rapidly expanding erythema surrounding ruptured cysts. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for secondary bacterial cellulitis and initiate prompt antibiotic therapy to prevent systemic spread and irreversible scarring, which could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face is throbbing, hot, and constantly leaking pus. I couldn't sleep at all because it hurts to even rest my head on the pillow."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced 48 hours ago with the rapid enlargement of several deep facial nodules. Over the past 12 hours, the lesions have become exquisitely tender and spontaneously ruptured, yielding purulent discharge. The patient reports a background of open and closed comedones on the face and upper trunk, with intermittent erythematous papules and pustules. The current nodulocystic lesions are uncharacteristically severe, producing pronounced localized pain and psychosocial distress. There is noted exacerbation following a recent period of high stress and increased sebum production resulting in noticeably oily skin.
Functional Status: Independent for activities of daily living, currently sleep-deprived.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychosocial distress and social withdrawal secondary to facial appearance and pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, guarding the face during conversation, fatigued appearance.
Physical Exam: Facial skin demonstrates extensive seborrhoea with open and closed comedones. There is a confluent distribution of erythematous papules and pustules across the forehead, cheeks, and upper trunk. The bilateral lower cheeks display grouped, 1-2 cm tender nodulocystic lesions with localized fluctuance and focal purulent weeping. The surrounding skin exhibits a 3 cm rim of reactive erythema without distinct advancing borders or regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Clean living space, appropriate heating, no trip hazards identified.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has been using over-the-counter benzoyl peroxide 5% inconsistently. No current oral antibiotics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Nodulocystic Acne with focal acute inflammation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Facial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a spreading, sharply demarcated erythematous plaque, lack of systemic toxicity, and normal vital signs aside from a low-grade reactive temperature.

Staphylococcal Folliculitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of classic comedones and deep cystic structures characteristic of acne rather than superficial hair follicle infections.

Rosacea (Papulopustular) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of comedones, truncal involvement, and lack of fixed central facial erythema or telangiectasias.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing exacerbation of nodulocystic acne characterized by severe focal pain, sleep disruption, and purulent rupture. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the rapidly spreading erythema and severe pain as potential facial cellulitis. On-site clinical evaluation confirms severe localized inflammatory acne rather than an invasive soft tissue infection; however, the degree of inflammation and pain necessitates immediate commencement of oral antibiotics and topical therapies to arrest the inflammatory cascade, manage acute pain, and prevent impending hypertrophic scarring. Delaying assessment and intervention until the next available routine appointment would result in prolonged severe pain and unacceptable cosmetic and psychological morbidity.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commence oral Doxycycline 100mg daily. Prescribe topical Clindamycin 1% lotion. Advise simple analgesia (Ibuprofen 400mg with food PRN).
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying a warm, damp washcloth to the painful cysts for 10 minutes every evening, you will encourage natural drainage and significantly reduce the throbbing pressure. Consistently taking the antibiotic with a large glass of water and remaining upright for 30 minutes will prevent esophageal irritation while successfully clearing the bacteria causing the painful flare.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess antibiotic response and discuss long-term dermatological management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a fever over 38.5C, chills, rapidly spreading redness across the cheeks or towards the eyes, visual changes, or if the pain becomes completely unmanageable with prescribed medication.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive based on current clinical findings. Skin infections can evolve rapidly.
Clinical Course: The acute pain and purulence should begin to improve within 48 to 72 hours of starting the antibiotic. The underlying acne will require weeks to months of consistent treatment to fully resolve.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical care if redness spreads to your eyelids, you develop visual changes, or if a high fever over 38.5C occurs within the next 24 hours.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The oral antibiotic will reduce the bacteria trapped in the deep cysts and lower the overall inflammation, relieving the acute pressure and pain.
Actionable Steps: Take the doxycycline exactly as prescribed to ensure the infection clears. Use the warm compresses as instructed to gain control over the local swelling and pain.

